8408203125, -0.0158233642578125, -0.01280975341796875, 0.017333984375, -8.106231689453125e-06, 0.039337158203125, -0.003612518310546875, 0.004985809326171875, -0.0062408447265625, 0.04315185546875, 0.0183258056640625, 0.00812530517578125, 0.01812744140625, -0.0250701904296875, -0.0099945068359375, 0.00771331787109375, -0.01177978515625, -0.0230560302734375, 0.017791748046875, 0.01306915283203125, 0.0433349609375, -0.01546478271484375, 0.01013946533203125, -0.0015954971313476562, -0.00568389892578125, 0.01482391357421875, -0.019561767578125, 0.00923919677734375, -0.0222930908203125, -0.0279388427734375, 0.04437255859375, -0.024017333984375, -0.022552490234375, 0.0186614990234375, -0.002719879150390625, 0.01302337646484375, 0.0200653076171875, 0.05145263671875, 0.043121337890625, -0.01045989990234375, -0.0216522216796875, 0.0016088485717773438], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "\"\"\"\nPurpose: To develop and evaluate a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for radiology report structuring and quality assurance, with the aim of supporting standardized reporting and improving report quality in clinical practice.\n\nMaterials and Methods: This retrospective study included 638 radiology reports from CT examinations of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists in 2023 and 2024. The multi-agent AI pipeline consisted of three sequential agents: (1) a structuring agent that first segmented reports into standardized anatomical sections at the sentence level using regex rules and local large language models; (2) a consistency-checking agent that then detected mismatches between the Findings and Impression sections or within sections; and (3) a QA agent that subsequently identified gender-anatomy conflicts and undocumented communication of critical findings using rule-based and language model prompts. The output of each agent was passed to the next, and the system\u2019s structuring and QA functions were evaluated together as a unified workflow. For radiologist evaluation, a random subset of 45 reports was selected using stratified random sampling to represent the distribution of body regions and types of flagged issues in the full dataset. Two board-certified radiologists independently reviewed these reports. \u201cCorrectly restructured\u201d was defined as accurate anatomical section assignment for the entire report without altering original content; \u201cincorrectly restructured\u201d indicated misassignment or loss of information at the report level; \u201cdisagreement\u201d reflected differing reviewer assessments per report. Inter-rater agreement was calculated using Cohen\u2019s kappa.\n\nResults: The multi-agent system structured the Findings sections of all 638 reports (22,270 sentences) into a predefined anatomical format while retaining the original report content. The system flagged 90 of 638 reports (14.1%, 95% CI: 11.5\u201317.0%), most commonly for section mismatches (80 of 638 reports, 12.5%, 95% CI: 10.1\u201315.4%). Gender-anatomy conflicts were detected in 5 of 638 reports (0.8%), and undocumented communication of critical findings in 3 of 638 reports (0.5%). In the radiologist evaluation, both reviewers agreed that 31 of 45 reports (69%, 95% CI: 53\u201382%) were correctly restructured, 2 of 45 reports (4%, 95% CI: 0\u201313%) were incorrectly restructured, and there was disagreement on 12 of 45 reports (27%, 95% CI: 15\u201342%). Cohen\u2019s kappa for agreement was 0.62, indicating substantial agreement. Both reviewers independently assessed whether any clinically important information was omitted or any fabricated content was introduced, using an open-ended review of each report; both agreed that neither occurred. Overall QA performance was rated as \"excellent\" or \"good\" in 38 of 45 reports (84%, 95% CI: 70\u201393%), with the remaining reports rated as \"fair\".\n\nConclusion: A locally deployed multi-agent AI system combined radiology report structuring and quality assurance within a single workflow, demonstrating favorable performance in radiologist evaluation. Limitations include the retrospective design, single-institution setting, and limited sample size for radiologist evaluation. Future work may explore generalizability to other institutions or imaging modalities. The study was conducted with institutional review board approval and in compliance with data privacy regulations. Such systems may support standardization of reporting and quality assurance in radiology practice.\n\"\"\""}}